[38;5;231m⏺[39m I looked before editing, and the premise doesn't hold: [38;5;153mclient.py[39m isn't staging-only.

  [38;5;153mclient.py:11,17[39m are the only two [38;5;153mrequests[39m calls, and both scripts import them:

  - [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py:5[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.staging.example[39m (the self-signed one)
  - [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py:5[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m — the nightly finance export

  So [38;5;153mverify=False[39m in [38;5;153mclient.py[39m silently disables certificate validation on the production export too, leaving that
  traffic open to interception with no error and no log line. That's the consequence I want a yes on before touching it.

  Two narrower options that fix staging without touching prod:

  1. [1mTrust the staging cert[0m — point [38;5;153mREQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m (or a [38;5;153mverify=<path>[39m default read from env) at the self-signed
     cert file. Verification stays on everywhere; staging just gets its CA. This is what I'd recommend.
  2. [1mEnv-gated opt-out[0m — [38;5;153mVERIFY = os.environ.get("REPORTS_VERIFY_TLS", "1") != "0"[39m, passed as [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY[39m, with only
     [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m setting it to [38;5;153m0[39m. Prod keeps verification by default.

  Or, if you want the blanket [38;5;153mverify=False[39m in [38;5;153mclient.py[39m anyway knowing it covers the prod export, say so and I'll make
  that exact change.

